<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

	<PropertyGroup>
		<TargetFrameworks>net6.0;net7.0;net8.0;net9.0;net10.0</TargetFrameworks>
		<Nullable>enable</Nullable>
		<ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
		<GenerateDocumentationFile>true</GenerateDocumentationFile>

		<!-- Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection.Abstractions 10.0.11 declares net6.0/net7.0
		     as "not tested" (both are EOL runtimes) but its assembly is netstandard2.0-compatible
		     and functions correctly there: verified via an actual runtime smoke test (DI container
		     built, AddAutoMapperLite resolved, a real Map<T> call executed and asserted) on net6.0,
		     not just assumed. Suppressed as a known-benign policy warning, not a real break. -->
		<SuppressTfmSupportBuildWarnings>true</SuppressTfmSupportBuildWarnings>

		<!-- NuGet Metadata -->
		<PackageId>AutoMapperLite</PackageId>
		<Version>4.4.0</Version>
		<PackageReleaseNotes>
			AutoMapperLite 4.4.0 — current official release; all prior versions are deprecated.

			Mapping engine and performance redesign. Each source/destination type pair now
			compiles into a cached delegate via System.Linq.Expressions on first use, instead of
			interpreting mappings through reflection. Dispatch, collection element resolution, and
			nested same-name property mapping are all resolved once and cached rather than
			re-resolved per call. See docs/performance.html for full measured results.

			• Added: IMapper.Map&lt;TSource, TDestination&gt;(TSource source) — a strongly-typed
			  overload for call sites where the source type is known at compile time. This is the
			  fastest way to call AutoMapperLite.
			• Added: collection destinations are no longer limited to List&lt;T&gt;.
			  Map&lt;TDestination&gt;(object) now also accepts T[], IList&lt;T&gt;, ICollection&lt;T&gt;,
			  IReadOnlyList&lt;T&gt;, IReadOnlyCollection&lt;T&gt;, and IEnumerable&lt;T&gt;. The same
			  flexibility applies to same-name nested collection properties.
			• Behavior change (disclosed): exceptions raised during mapping now propagate directly
			  as InvalidOperationException instead of being wrapped in TargetInvocationException.
			• Behavior change (disclosed): List&lt;T&gt; mapping resolves the item map from the source
			  collection's declared item type rather than each element's runtime type.
			• Allocations for simple, medium, nested, and deeply-nested object mapping now match
			  hand-written manual mapping exactly. Collection mapping allocation is within a few
			  percent of manual mapping/Mapster at any size, from 10 items to 100,000.
			• No public API breaking changes since 4.0.2 (only for anyone implementing IMapper
			  directly rather than using the built-in Mapper class, due to the new interface
			  member above).

			Previous (4.0.2):
			• Fixed: updated test tooling required net8.0+; the test project now targets
			  net8.0-net10.0 (the library itself still targets net6.0-net10.0).
			• Fixed: Activator.CreateInstance failures now throw a clear InvalidOperationException
			  naming the type, instead of a bare MissingMethodException.
			• Added real, measured BenchmarkDotNet comparisons against manual mapping, AutoMapper,
			  and Mapster.
			• Verified and documented: null source properties are skipped (not assigned), auto-map
			  requires an exact type match, and records with init-only properties map correctly.
		</PackageReleaseNotes>
		<Authors>Atik Hassan</Authors>
		<Company>AutoMapperLite</Company>
		<Description>AutoMapperLite is a lightweight object-to-object mapper for .NET — a small, dependency-minimal alternative to AutoMapper for mapping DTOs and entities. Each source/destination type pair is compiled into a cached delegate via System.Linq.Expressions on first use, rather than interpreted via reflection on every call. Targets net6.0 through net10.0. Features fluent CreateMap/ForMember/ForPath profiles, nested object and List&lt;T&gt; collection mapping, thread-safe configuration, and singleton-friendly dependency injection via AddAutoMapperLite. Built for projects that want a simple, dependency-minimal mapper without a large footprint; see the published benchmarks for exactly how it compares to AutoMapper and Mapster. Official site: https://automapperlite.atkhssn.info</Description>
		<PackageTags>mapper;object mapper;object mapping;dto mapper;dto mapping;automapper alternative;mapster alternative;dotnet mapper;csharp mapper;lightweight mapper;expression mapper</PackageTags>
		<PackageLicenseExpression>MIT</PackageLicenseExpression>
		<PackageProjectUrl>https://automapperlite.atkhssn.info</PackageProjectUrl>
		<RepositoryUrl>https://github.com/atkhssn/AutoMapperLite</RepositoryUrl>
		<RepositoryType>git</RepositoryType>
		<Copyright>© 2025-2026 Atik Hassan</Copyright>
		<PackageReadmeFile>README.md</PackageReadmeFile>
		<PackageIcon>icon.png</PackageIcon>
	</PropertyGroup>

	<ItemGroup>
		<!-- Files to include in NuGet -->
		<None Include="README.md" Pack="true" PackagePath="" />
		<None Include="LICENSE" Pack="true" PackagePath="" />
		<None Include="icon.png" Pack="true" PackagePath="" />
	</ItemGroup>

	<ItemGroup>
		<PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection.Abstractions" Version="10.0.11" />
	</ItemGroup>

	<ItemGroup>
		<InternalsVisibleTo Include="AutoMapperLite.Tests" />
	</ItemGroup>

	<ItemGroup>
		<Compile Remove="AutoMapperLite.Tests\**" />
		<Compile Remove="AutoMapperLite.Benchmarks\**" />
		<EmbeddedResource Remove="AutoMapperLite.Tests\**" />
		<EmbeddedResource Remove="AutoMapperLite.Benchmarks\**" />
		<None Remove="AutoMapperLite.Tests\**" />
		<None Remove="AutoMapperLite.Benchmarks\**" />
	</ItemGroup>

</Project>